<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Machynlleth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Machynlleth is a market town in Powys, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, in the Dyfi valley. In 1404 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a> held a parliament there, where he is said to have been crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> in the presence of envoys from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>. It is sometimes called the ancient capital of Wales.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/machynlleth.jpg" alt="Machynlleth" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Celuici · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Central_Machynlleth,_June_2016.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Machynlleth is a market town, community and electoral ward in Powys, within the historic boundaries of Montgomeryshire. It lies in the Dyfi Valley at the intersection of the A487 and A489 roads. The name derives from ma, meaning field or plain, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cynllaith">Cynllaith</a>, a personal name. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/radiocarbon-dating">Radiocarbon dating</a> in the late 1990s showed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> was mined in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> within a mile of the town centre. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> built a fort at Cefn Caer, near Pennal, and are reputed to have had another, called Maglona, at Machynlleth.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>One of the earliest written references is the royal charter of 1291 in which Edward I granted Owen de la Pole, Lord of Powys, a market every Wednesday and two fairs a year. The market and fair thrived, and in 1613 other towns complained that they were damaging their cloth trade. The Dyfi Bridge was first mentioned in 1533. In 1404 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/owain-glyndwr">Owain Glyndŵr</a>, who rebelled against the English in the reign of Henry IV, held a parliament at Machynlleth and was crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> near the Parliament House, one of three medieval houses in the town, in the presence of foreign envoys.</p><p>For this the town claims to be the ancient capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, although it has never had official recognition as one. Glyndŵr held his last parliament at nearby Pennal. By local tradition the Royal House held the Welsh ally of the English kings Dafydd Gam prisoner from 1404 to 1412.</p><p>In the English Civil War Rowland Pugh of Mathafarn supported the Royalist side and ambushed a Parliamentarian force under Sir Thomas Myddleton on 2 November 1644; on 29 November Myddleton burned Mathafarn and several houses in the town. In the nineteenth century Plas Machynlleth was the home of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derry">Londonderry</a> family, whose townspeople-funded clock tower, designed by Henry Kennedy of Bangor, became the symbol of the town. The Corris Railway carried slate from 1859 to 1948, and Laura Ashley opened her first shop in the town in 1961.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Machynlleth has a population of 2,098. Welsh is spoken alongside English: in the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Census</a> 62 per cent of residents had some knowledge of it. The town hosted the National Eisteddfod in 1937 and 1981 and applied unsuccessfully for city status in 2000 and 2002. It lies in the Dyfi Biosphere, designated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> in 2009, and near the Centre for Alternative Technology, and it is home to MoMA Wales, an arts centre that began in 1986 in an old chapel.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Machynlleth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By 1404 Glyndwr held most of Wales, took Harlech and Aberystwyth castles, and summoned parliaments at <b>Machynlleth</b> and Harlech.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-wales-under-owain-glyndwr">Principality of Wales under Owain Glyndwr</a></li></ul></section>
